    "prompt": "In the grid, each small square has side of length 1. What is the minimum distance from 'Start' to 'Finish' travelling only on edges or diagonals of the squares?",
    "image": "qimages/ukmt/pink-2015/q09.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$2\\sqrt{2}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{2}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$2+2\\sqrt{2}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$4\\sqrt{2}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "6"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Use diagonals",
        "body": "Moving along a diagonal of a unit square costs $\\sqrt2<2$, so diagonals are efficient. From Start (top left) to Finish (bottom right) of the $4\\times2$ grid we must go 4 across and 2 down. Two diagonals cover 2 across and 2 down, leaving 2 across along edges. Total $2\\sqrt2+2$.\n\n**Answer: (C) $2+2\\sqrt2$**"
      }
    ],
