    "wrongPoints": null,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "We use the notation $\\overline{ab}$ for the two-digit number with digits $a$ and $b$. Let $a, b, c$ be different digits. How many ways can you choose the digits $a, b, c$ such that $\\overline{ab} < \\overline{bc} < \\overline{ca}$?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "84"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "96"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "504"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "729"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "1000"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Digits must increase",
        "body": "$\\overline{ab}<\\overline{bc}$ forces $a<b$ (the digits are different), and $\\overline{bc}<\\overline{ca}$ forces $b<c$. So $a<b<c$ with $a\\ge1$ (leading digits are nonzero). Any three different nonzero digits can be put in increasing order in exactly one way, so the count is $\\binom{9}{3}=84$.\n\n**Answer: (A) 84**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "counting"
