    "wrongPoints": null,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Ten different numbers (not necessarily integers) are written down. Any number that is equal to the product of the other nine numbers is then underlined. At most, how many numbers can be underlined?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "0"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "2"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "9"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "10"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Two is possible, three is not",
        "body": "Two is possible: write $1,-1,-2,\\tfrac12,3,\\tfrac13,4,\\tfrac14,5,\\tfrac15$. The product of the other nine for $1$ is $(-1)(-2)\\tfrac12\\cdot1\\cdot1\\cdot1=1$, and for $-1$ it is $1\\cdot(-2)\\tfrac12=-1$. For three, suppose $a,b,c$ are underlined and $N$ is the product of the other seven. Then $a=bcN$, $b=acN$, $c=abN$. Substituting gives $cN=\\pm1$; $cN=1$ forces $a=b$, and $cN=-1$ forces $a=-b$ and then $c=\\pm b$ so two numbers are equal: a contradiction.\n\n**Answer: (C) 2**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
